kauer@pcug.org.au (Karl Auer) writes:

>>          The right of access to any publicly available information,
>>          restricted only by financial constraints.

>Everything after the first comma is irrelevant IMHO. Financial constraints are 
>a constant.

The intention of the sentence is to address the other constraints that
might be placed on such information: that it doesn't meet a certain
moral standard, that it offends certain political sensibilities, that
it embarrases certain powerful people, or that it empowers the reader in
ways that are difficult to control.

The mention of financial contraints is also intended to address those who
would like to use the document to demand equal and limitless bandwidth
for all people; that is a very common view among new users of the net, 
who don't understand how it has been constructed or how financial 
constraints are related to "real" costs. Such folks are more than happy 
to splurge home-videos across saturated links, and very willing to squawk 
"freedom of speech!" when user charges are proposed to control such abuse.

>>          The right to control and license intellectual property invested
>>          in original expressions, but not in algorithms nor other
>>          abstract specifications.

>It is dangerous to make rules that may conflict with existing law. Like it or 
>not, ths above does not reflect the current state of law in many 
>jurisdictions. If you left out everything after and including "invested" no 
>conflict would exist. The struggle to make the rest of the sentence not 
>conflict is another battle. On the whole, I find this right rather irrelevant.

This right would be irrelevant without the "invested ...". Why have it?

The first paragraph of the ERR reads:

   "The intention of this document is to provide an ethical standard with
   which to measure the policies of states and corporations with regard
   to the Internet and related multicast communications networks."
   
If the ERR suggests that existing laws are unethical, then one of two outcomes 
will ensue; either the document will be useful in changing these laws, or it 
will be disregarded. It's not possible to say which outcome will ensue, but 
the ERR has to take a stand one way or the other on the issue of software 
patents, or it will be a very incomplete document. It so happens that 
Australia has no algorithmic patents, and most developers here vehemently
oppose them, so that's the way that the ERR goes.

But, of course, if you would prefer an ethical statement that smiles on 
software patents, you are quite free to construct one. 

>>          The right to receive any information from any source.

>I find this unclear - it is too easy to interpret it as a right to demand that 
>someone else transmits the desired information. Perhaps something like "the 
>right to receive information transmitted from any source"?

I think that the right is clear as it is stated. I also can't see how you can
receive information that isn't "transmitted", so I don't think that the
alternate phrasing helps.

>>          The right of every person to restrict access by any means to
>>          any information that they originate or receive.

>"By any means" is a red rag to the legal bull. I'd drop that phrase. Is 
>murder, for example, an acceptable means to prevent a person accessing 
>something?

Good point. Given the other right to "transform" what you receive, I think
that the "by any means" here can be dropped. 

>>          The right to ignore information of any nature provided that
>>          this ignorance does not infringe upon the rights of others.

>How can my ignoring information possibly infringe on the rights of others? A 
>genuine question :-)

Copyright notices ...

>>          The right to seek legal recourse for damages caused by the
>>          actions and expressions of others.

>Seems redundant - this depends on, and is well codified in, existing law.

The ERR needs to acknowledge such recourses as possible consequences of the 
freedoms it upholds, or else it will be viewed as an extreme position. Without
this clause it could easily be interpreted as recommending against any sort of
libel laws. 

>>          The right, as administrator of a system or as moderator of a
>>          moderated forum, to deny participation in that system or forum
>>          to any person for any reason.

>This may well run foul of anti-discrimination law. Needs much more thought, I 
>think!

See my recent reply to Carl Kadie in this thread.

>>          The responsibility to neither harrass nor vilify others on the
>>          basis of race, religion, ethnic background, nationality,
>>          gender, political affiliation, sexual preference, age or
>>          disability.

>It seems simpler to drop everything after "others". Don't you think?

All flames are unethical? No, I don't think so.

>>          The responsibility to represent yourself, your observations and
>>          opinions, and the expressions of others sincerely and without
>>          misrepresentation.

>Why not just replace everything after "sincerely" with "accurately"?

Because then you might suggest I am unethical if you don't agree with my
paraphrasing or inferences. 

>>          The responsibility to observe the rights and responsibilities
>>          above, and to educate others about them.

>I don't like the bit about educating others. There are too many unpleasantly 
>evangelist religions about for me to accept that it is anyone's duty to 
>educate except where the potential student is seeking and requesting 
>education.

If we don't have a responsibility to promote ethical behaviour, who does?

>In general, a good effort! My last (nitpicky) point is that some of the righst 
>start with "the right of every person" and some start with "the right" - I  
>think the latter is better.

I like the latter better too, but sometimes it is not adequate. I only use
the former where the latter is unclear.

