>While it is commendable for sysadmins to use due process, etc, to
>"mandate" due process would arguably be interfering with the
>individual administration styles and policies of sysadmins.

I'm not recommending that due process and fairness be required by the
Statement, only that the Statement not exclude due process and
fairness.

For example, suppose an organization like a university decides that
students should not be treated arbitrary. Suppose, it decides that
students should have hearing before being punished. Suppose, it
guarantees such due process in its contract with students.
Then the statement:

>> >          The right, as administrator of a system or as moderator of a
>> >          moderated forum, to deny participation in that system or forum
>> >          to any person for any reason.

is not true because a sys admin at such a university would have a
contractual obligation to provide students with due process.

I understand that there are emergencies. University contracts that
I've read (U.S. ones) cover that too. I understand that a private BBS
owner seldom enters into a contract that promises due process.

As I said in my previous article, I think the fix is easy. Just change
the last line to "any person for any *legal* reason."

