~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
EYE.NET                                                        EYE.NET

       EVIL KARLA FILE JUST KEEPS GOING AND GOING AND GOING ...

                                  by
                             K.K. CAMPBELL


What the hell is it with schools and the stupid Karla Homolka FAQ that
circulates cyberspace?

The Damn Thing just won't go away.

A "FAQ" (pronounced fak) is a text file of "Frequently Asked Questions"
on a subject. The Homolka FAQ (which you can get in our web site, among
other places) answers questions like: "Who the hell is Karla Homolka?"
After all, the net is global -- many netters have no idea.

The Damn Thing seems to bring out the righteous cop in academic staff.
Last May, eye ran a cover story about the London (Ontario) Police
Department goosestepping into the life of a University of Western
Ontario student. The cops got the complete support of Western comp
security goon Reg Quinton. The L.P.D.- UWO tag-team forced the student
into opening all his computer directories to prying eyes and forwarding
his future email to the police -- just because the student had helped
edit the Homolka FAQ.

To hear Quinton explain himself afterward in Usenet, you'd think there
was a ban on Homolka rumors. Let's see ... Homolka's trial took
something like one (1) day -- this is notably more efficient than
O.J.'s trial. I will, thus, throw caution to the wind and surmise that
she pleaded guilty. Yeah, Homolka pleaded guilty. (Bailiff! Smelling
salts for the noblewomen in the balcony seats please!)

Last year, to underscore the stupidity of suggesting it is somehow
illegal to write that Homolka pleaded guilty after her super-quickie
trial, Ryerson journalism school boss John Miller published a similar
statement as a letter to The Ryersonian. That letter was then piped
into cyberspace. The justice system was brought to the brink of
collapse.

Universities aren't the only ones having fun. Sarah Rosenblatt
(comets@io. org), a Grade 12 student at Birchmount Park Collegiate
Institute, was hauled down to school offices in February because she
gave a copy of the FAQ to a friend. Since the FAQ has that titillating
aura of Forbidden Fruit, it fast made its way around the school. One
teen's parents found it, recoiled in fright and called the school. The
school vice-principal Brian Punchard called Rosenblatt in for the third
degree.

"I was called down during my second-period class quite unexpectedly,"
she told eyeNET. "I had no idea why. I didn't think I had done anything
worthy of an interview with a VP. He made me wait about 15 minutes. He
started out by asking me if I was on Internet. I was a bit suspicious
because I doubted that he even knew what the Internet was. And I sure
didn't understand why he would want to talk to me about it."

But he soon cut to the chase. "He finally asked if I'd heard about the
Homolka case on the Internet. I said I had, and then he told me that
they had found out that I had distributed the FAQ." She was asked to
"confess." She did.

Rosenblatt was ordered outside to wait on the bench again and Punchard
called her mother. "My mother later told me that he was trying to freak
her out about `computer crime' and `hackers' -- he actually used that
word relating to me.

"He told me the school superintendent didn't think I should be
suspended, but from the tone in his voice it seemed like he wanted to
suspend me. He told me that I would be suspended if this happened
again."

The school then undertook a locker search of Rosenblatt's friends, to
ferret out the evil document.

                            WEB DWEEB

Moses Moore (jajmoore.html@csclub.uwaterloo.ca) wrote eyeNET email in
response to our recent adoration of the truly useless web site: "Check
out Mark Tompsett's `Questionnaire For Women.' A questionnaire for
women to fill out so that they might qualify to be his mate ...
http://csclub.uwaterloo.ca/u/mtompset is where the fun starts."

And indeed it does! Update: Mark, a '90s kinda guy, says he has since
changed the name of his dating survey to "A questionnaire for people."
That means men are now OK! Excited at suddenly being included, I filled
out Mark's form and having been sitting anxiously by the phone!

The interminable questionnaire (done as a Netscape form) gets downright
personal. It actually asks for my phone number and home address. It may
have asked for my credit card numbers and SIN, too, but I'm not sure
because I started suffering low blood-sugar delirium around question
941.

You'll notice some questions are kind of leading. Like, for question
729 -- "Who do you think Jesus was?" -- you are given set responses: 1)
fictional/non-existent; 2) a man; 3) a good man; 4) a prophet; 5) a son
of God; or 6) the Son of God. There was no space for: 7) my mailman.
But that's OK, I carved it into the form through my monitor and I hope
Mark can read it clear enough because the glass was pretty tough and I
only had a set of keys to work with.

              BACHELORETTE #2: DO YOU USE SLIP OR PPP?

The World Wide Web Dating Game -- that's right. Perhaps Mark Tompsett
will find happiness at http://www.galcit.caltech.edu:80/~aure/date/.

You fill out a form to be a contestant -- which helps "stream"
candidates somewhat. If your name comes up for a game, they send you
mail asking that you send a digital picture of yourself and the
festivities begin. A "game" involves an entrant choosing between three
contestants -- just like the old, horribly- embarrassing TV version.
After the date, entrants are to report back on their date.

I'd have entered but feared finding myself in a situation where I'd
have to pick between Doctress Neutopia, John L. Brewer and Mother
Foundress. Besides, I'm still waiting by the phone for Mark to call ...

                    UNDER THE COBWEBS ... WEB SITES!

Everyone maintains "hotlists" of web sites. Hotlists are places you
regularly visit. It was inevitable someone would think of doing the
direct opposite. Enter the U.K.'s Stephen Turner (sret1@cam.ac.uk) and
his "Coldlist": "These are sites which are of very little interest to
almost everybody!" he writes.

It's an awesome collection: the entertaining Board Appointments at the
Alberta Research Council page; the sexy Western Interstate Commission
for Higher Education Demographic Analysis Tool page; and don't miss the
League Table for Division 3, Group 2 of the Finnish Soccer League site.

All at http://www.statslab. cam.ac.uk/~sret1/coldlist.html .

                                  
