    I started using the internet only a little more than a year ago,
and I have been so preoccupied with the computer-related activities
that this new extension of my powers has made possible for me that I
have had little opportunity to comment on two aspects of the internet
that struck me with great force when I began using it, and which the
ensuing months have not made any more acceptable to me.

   The first of these is the notion of "freedom" as the complete
absence of codified and actionable rules, an attitude that seems to me
to permeate the discussions in precisely those of the newsgroups that
I follow which are frequented most by persons who present themselves
as having, or having had, the most important roles in the development
of the internet.

  The notion of "freedom" as the absence of codified and actionable
rules is infantile and must inevitably lead to a totalitarian regime
in which each individual is assigned a "place" deemed appropriate by
those whose "place" it is to deem things appropriate.

  The second aspect of the internet that offended me early on is the
near-total absence of women in the newsgroups that I follow.

  I do not much like to have to spend any great length of time in any
environment in which I hear nothing but the male point of view, but
the quality of discourse in these male-only newsgroups is truly
abysmal, amounting very often to nothing more than instant verbal
gratification, as if a conspicuous display of the systematic
willingness to refuse to communicate, and ignorance of the fact that
writing is a matter of discovering what you believe, were somehow
"manly" attributes.

  The internet must belong to all of the people on this planet because
otherwise there will be nothing to prevent the enormous integrative
potential of the internet infrastructure from being turned into its
opposite, and I therefore applaud all of the fledgling efforts at
hammering out a Constitution for the internet.

  As precedent, I might suggest the precursor of the First Amendment
to the U.S. Constitution that is found in Article I, Section 6 [17
Sept 1787], which states:

  "The Senators and Representatives ... shall in all cases, except
Treason, Felony, and Breach of the Peace, be privileged from Arrest
during their Attendance at the Session of their respective Houses, and
in going to and returning from the same; and for any Speech or Debate
in either House, they shall not be questioned in any other place."

  Unless this discussion is well-rooted in similarly fundamental and
practical matters of Right, it will fall afoul of the fallacy that
currently rules the internet, namely, that he who knows the most about
computers, say, or physics, or some similarly philosophically-inert
subject automatically knows the most about everything else!

  If, as (then-candidate) Albert Gore wrote in his famous Scientific
American article, we confront a future that none of us can clearly
conceive today, then the very least we can do today is to take utmost
care that the policy which will govern that future and which it is our
inescapable responsibility to formulate rests on the deepest granite
shelves of history we can find.

